    "Battlements are protruding facilities of a fortress built at constant intervals to defend the area against the enemy forces. \r\n\r\nIn Korean, these battlements are called &quot;&quot;Chiseong,&quot;&quot; where &quot;&quot;Chi&quot;&quot; means a &quot;&quot;pheasant,&quot;&quot; which is exceptional at hiding itself from potential predators. It is speculated that there were 5 battlements in the Heunginjimun and Gwanghuimun areas. Traces of battlements were found during the excavation work performed in the site of Dongdaemun Stadium and they underwent restoration before being displayed at the Dongdaemun History & Culture Park. ": 1,
    "While walking along Seoul City Wall from Cheongun Park toward the Inwangsan Mountain Trail, you will come across a crossroad that leads to the peak of Inwangsan Mountain and Gichabawi Rock. Gichabawi Rock was named so because when it was viewed from far away, it appeared as though there are a couple of trains here. Of particular note, Inwangsan Gokseong presents picturesque scenery along the ridge of the Bawisan of Inwangsan that exhibits rugged terrains. Gichabawi Rock is where you can also observe the excellent fortification techniques of the Joseon Dynasty that enabled the construction of a city wall upon rugged terrains. ": 1,
    "Location: outside the wall - near the Dongdaemun Seonggwak Park (site of Dongdaemun Church)\r\nNumber: 61\r\n\r\nHunguk chaegeunggyeomdokyeokjangsibin sahanpiryeong ilpaejangjeolchungseosegak ipaejangjeolchungjeonsuseon sampaejangsagwayujehan seoksudobyeonsuoyuseon ilpaebyeonsuyangyu(ui) ipaebyeonsuhwangseungseon sampaebyeonsugimjeongnip ganghuisasionyeonsaworilgaechuk \r\n\r\nExplanation: In April 1706 (32nd year of King Sukjong&apos;s reign), Hunnyeondogam officers divided themselves into 3 groups to rebuild the city wall. 10 supervising officers and non-government officers such as Jeolchung and Sagwa commanded the 3 groups, and engineers including masons participated in the project. Sa Han Pil-yeong, 1 Paejang Jeolchung Seong Se-gak, 2 Paejang Jeolchung Jeon Su-seon, 3 Paejang Sagwa Yu je-han, Mason Dopyeonsu O Yu-seon, 1 Pae Pyeonsu Yang Yuk (Ui), 2 Pae Pyeongsu Kim Jeong-rip were in charge of the reconstruction project. ": 1,
    "Location: Inside the wall\r\nNumber: 142\r\n\r\nGapsiguworil ganyeokhangungnyang gamgwanyangukjwa byeonsuijunggon\r\n\r\nExplanation: In Sept. 1824 (24th year of Sunjo), Ganyeok Han Guk-ryang, Gamgwan Yang Guk-jwa and Pyeonsu Yi Jung-gon were involved in the repair work of the low wall. ": 1,
    "When you walk along the Namsan Beltway extending from the Main Hall Hae to Namsan (Mongmyeoksan) Mountain, you will see a site marking the area which once contained the fortress. Follow that mark and you will see the Wooden Stairway amid the dense forest and shrubs. \r\n\r\nThe original appearance of Seoul City Wall, built during the reign of King Taejo remains intact. The section of the wall next to the wooden stairway, which runs along the eastern ridge of Namsan, still looks like it did 600 years ago. ": 1,
    "Gwanghuimun, located southeast of Seoul City Wall, was called Sigumun (“Gate of coffins”) because the bodies of the deceased were transported beyond the wall via this gate. \r\n\r\nPart of the gate was destroyed during the Japanese Colonial Period, and in the 1960’s, during a street expansion project, the remaining part was dismantled. Then, in 1975, the gate was rebuilt at the current location, some 15m south of its original position.\r\n\r\nPeople were reluctant to use &quot;&quot;Sigumun&quot;&quot; because of what it symbolized, but there was a king who actually passed through this gate. It was King Injo, who escaped through here to Namhansanseong Fortress in 1636, during the invasion by the Manchus. \r\n\r\nSindan-dong is also a designation related to Gwanghuimun. The houses of shamans who gave comfort to the souls of the deceased were situated outside this gate, so the area was named Sindang (“Shaman House”)-ri. However, it was renamed Sindang(“New House”)-ri later. ": 1,
    "Location: Inside the wall - near Cheongundae\r\nNumber: 4\r\n\r\nGayeonggunyeon gapjasiworil paejangojaemin gamgwanidonghan byeonsuyongseonghwi \r\n\r\nExplanation: In Oct. 1804 (4th year of King Sunjo&apos;s reign), Paejang Oh Jae-min, Gamgwan Yi Dong-han and Pyeonsu Yong Seong-hwi were involved in the repair work. ": 1,
    "There is a road on the side of the Cheongun Park near the northern entrance of Inwangsan Mountain. Along the Inwangsan Mountain Trail from Cheongun Park near Yun Dong-ju Hill toward Inwangsan Mountain, you will come across a hiking trail behind an iron gate. There are military facilities in the area, so you may be requested to present your ID card to explore the area. ": 1,
    "Location: Inside the wall - Beneath the first low wall of Gwanghuimun Gate \r\nNumber: 63\r\n\r\nChisogamgwangimdeokhyeong seowonijinyeop gojikgimgwangbok\r\n\r\nExplanation: Chiso (in charge of Administrative Affairs) Gamgwan Kim Deok-hyeong, Seowon (Working-level Administrator) Yi Jin-yeop, and Gojik (Storage Keeper) Kim Gwang-bok were in charge of the fortification work ": 1,
    "The Seoul City Wall Historic Site Exhibition Hall displays the ruins of the fortress wall revealed through excavation surveys from 2013 to 2014.\r\n\r\nThis site is part of the Namsan section of Hanyangdoseong Fortress, and is a section of the fortress wall that has been thought to have been lost.\r\n\r\nNamsan Mountain, where the exhibition hall is located, is a place that compresses the long history of Hanyangdoseong.\r\n\r\nIn the exhibition hall area, which covers the ruins of Hanyangdoseong (1396), the site of the Joseon Shrine distribution (1925), and the Namsan fountain (1969), you can see the history of the construction of the Joseon Dynasty, the hardships of Japanese colonial era, urbanization after liberation, and the recent excavation and maintenance process. ": 1
